{
  /* Worker CONTRACT TESTS, typechecked separately from the worker source.

     `sw/tsconfig.json` deliberately has `"types": []` and no DOM, so worker code
     cannot reference `window`/`document`/node globals that do not exist inside a
     worker. That strictness is the point, and it is also why the test file
     cannot live in that project: a bun test needs `bun:test`, and the fakes it
     builds for `Cache`/`CacheStorage`/`fetch` need the WebWorker lib as well.

     So the test gets its own project: WebWorker types (the APIs under test) plus
     the ambient bun types (the runner). Still no DOM — a test that reached for
     `document` would be testing something the worker can never do.

     Referenced from the root tsconfig so plain `tsc -b` covers it; otherwise
     this file would be typechecked by nothing, which is exactly the gap
     `sw/tsconfig.json`'s own header warns about. That reference is only possible
     because the tests import `policy.ts` rather than the worker entry: the entry
     pulls in the gitignored `precache.gen.ts`, and referencing anything that
     needs it would break `tsc -b` on a clean checkout. `composite` is required
     of every referenced project. */
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2023", "WebWorker"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "composite": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noEmit": true,
    "strict": true,
    "noUnusedLocals": true,
    "noUnusedParameters": true,
    "noFallthroughCasesInSwitch": true,
    "verbatimModuleSyntax": false
  },
  /* The tests plus exactly the sources they import. A composite project must
     list every file it compiles, so `policy.ts` and the release helper are named
     here rather than picked up implicitly — and `policy.ts` is checked under
     WebWorker types, the same way its own project checks it.

     `sw.ts` is NOT here (nor is `precache.gen.ts`): the entry imports generated
     code, so including it would reintroduce the clean-checkout failure this
     whole split exists to remove. `tsc -p sw` covers the entry after a build. */
  "include": ["./**/*.test.ts", "./policy.ts", "./notify.ts", "../scripts/release.ts"]
}
